# kJubilee — nightly broadcast schedule publish
#
# Install on the app host:
#   cp scripts/kjubilee-schedules.cron /etc/cron.d/kjubilee-schedules
#   chmod 644 /etc/cron.d/kjubilee-schedules
#   systemctl restart cron
#
# ── why 12:00 UTC and not just after midnight ───────────────────────────────
# The engine spec's own scheduler runs at 00:05 UTC, and that is the wrong hour
# for THIS job for two reasons:
#
#   1. Today's schedule has to exist BEFORE today starts. A job that generates
#      the current day at 00:05 leaves five minutes in which every listener
#      resolves against a file that is not there yet.
#   2. Tomorrow's file wants to be at the Cloudflare edge before the rollover,
#      or every client in the world fetches it from the origin within the same
#      minute.
#
# Running at midday solves both: today is already published (and republishing it
# is a no-op, since the generator is deterministic), tomorrow goes out twelve
# hours early and is warm at the edge by midnight, and the rest are spares.
#
# ── why SEVEN days and not three ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# It was --days 3, on the reasoning that a failed run costs nothing because two
# more days are already out. That is true of ONE failed run. Three consecutive
# failures — a box down over a long weekend, a token expiry nobody sees on a
# Friday, an R2 outage — and stations start going dark with no warning at all,
# because nothing about a missing day file is visible until a listener presses
# play. A week of runway costs four more small JSON files per station per night
# and turns a three-day silence into a six-day one nobody will ever reach.
#
# Prove it rather than assume it: `node tools/check-schedules.js 7` reports any
# on-air station without a populated day file, and exits non-zero.
#
# ── pools ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# --rebuild-pools is deliberately NOT set. Rebuilding reads the frame headers of
# every track off J:\kjubilee.com\music, which this host cannot see: pools are
# built on the workstation after an ingest and copied to tmp/pools/. A nightly
# job that tried to rebuild them would fail every night on a machine that has no
# music share. See ensurePool() in r2-publish-schedules.js.
#
SHELL=/bin/sh
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
MAILTO=""

0 12 * * * root cd /var/www/kjubilee.com && /usr/bin/node scripts/r2-publish-schedules.js --apply --days 7 >> /var/log/kjubilee-schedules.log 2>&1
